WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.587
Welcome to Primary Education Online. This
is the first of four sessions about

00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:14.113
understanding Autistic experience and
making primary school more accessible,

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:18.217
connected and welcoming. As we work
through the series, keep your own

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:22.623
classroom or school setting in view. We'll
begin with the lens we bring to

00:00:22.623 --> 00:00:27.391
students, then explore communication and
the school day. From there we'll design

00:00:27.391 --> 00:00:31.918
one practical change and prepare a
purposeful conversation with a student or

00:00:31.918 --> 00:00:36.384
family. You can listen through, or use the
pauses to make notes and try the

00:00:36.384 --> 00:00:41.272
activities. The recording leaves a short
pause each time; pause the video yourself

00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:45.859
whenever you would like longer. Have the
Primary Education Online Participant

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:50.748
Guide beside you if you can. Let's begin
with curiosity about students and what we

00:00:50.748 --> 00:00:52.136
can change around them.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.381
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands and waters from which we are

00:00:58.382 --> 00:01:02.537
joining this learning, and pay my respects
to Elders past and present. I

00:01:02.538 --> 00:01:06.986
recognise the continuing connections of
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander

00:01:06.987 --> 00:01:10.850
peoples to Country, culture and community.
I extend that respect to

00:01:10.851 --> 00:01:15.416
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
people taking part today. If you know the

00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:19.923
Country you are on, you might take a
moment to acknowledge it in your own way.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.020
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.160
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.120
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.560
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.760
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.770
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.880
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.510
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.380
Australia’s colonial history

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.090
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.520
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

00:02:07.121 --> 00:02:11.513
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

00:02:11.514 --> 00:02:14.039
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.669
There is no single right way to take part.
Listening is participation. Private

00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:28.078
notes count, and passing is always an
option. If you are joining colleagues

00:02:28.078 --> 00:02:33.780
online, your camera can be on or off. You
can use the chat, a reaction, or your

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.335
voice when that works for you. Please
move, stretch or step away whenever you

00:02:39.335 --> 00:02:44.818
need to. The original workshop includes
some small group activities. In this

00:02:44.818 --> 00:02:50.081
recording, every activity can be done
privately. If you are watching with

00:02:50.081 --> 00:02:55.417
colleagues, you can pause and talk
together, but you won't need a breakout

00:02:55.417 --> 00:03:01.192
room to follow the learning. Please use
initials or roles only when you think or

00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:06.528
talk about students. A Year Two student is
enough. Leave out names, school

00:03:06.528 --> 00:03:11.937
details and anything that could identify a
family. We're modelling the same

00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:17.346
choices we want students to have: more
than one way to communicate, time to

00:03:17.346 --> 00:03:20.709
think, and permission to look after
themselves.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.479
Let's put some real students at the centre
of this work. Turn to page four of

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.719
your Participant Guide. Bring two or three
students to mind, ideally students

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.372
who are quite different from one another.
They might be different ages, communicate

00:03:38.373 --> 00:03:43.612
differently, or experience the same
classroom routine in very different ways.

00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:48.714
Label them Student A, Student B and
Student C. What strengths, interests or

00:03:48.715 --> 00:03:54.229
moments of connection should stay visible
as we learn? Perhaps there is something

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:59.607
they know a great deal about, a way they
notice patterns, or a moment when they

00:03:59.608 --> 00:04:05.054
seem especially at ease. Then write what
brings you here. What would you like to

00:04:05.055 --> 00:04:10.155
understand better? Keep these notes
private. You do not need to explain why

00:04:10.156 --> 00:04:15.395
you chose these students. Pause here for a
couple of minutes if you'd like to

00:04:15.396 --> 00:04:18.152
write, then come back when you're ready.

00:04:26.161 --> 00:04:31.146
Keep that page handy. We will return to
these same students across all four

00:04:31.147 --> 00:04:36.603
sessions. The point is to build a broader
picture, so that one person's experience

00:04:36.604 --> 00:04:41.926
doesn't become our template for everyone.
It's fine to have questions you cannot

00:04:41.927 --> 00:04:46.036
answer yet. Those questions give us
somewhere useful to begin.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.152
We're going to hear from Autistic young
people now. Let's make room for their

00:04:52.153 --> 00:04:56.503
accounts in their own words. Listen for
what each speaker wants others to

00:04:56.504 --> 00:05:01.035
understand. You don't need to diagnose,
compare a speaker with a student you

00:05:01.036 --> 00:05:05.628
teach, or find one lesson that explains
everyone. Each account belongs to the

00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:10.160
person sharing it. We'll watch the
supplied film in full, then have a moment

00:05:10.161 --> 00:05:10.825
to reflect.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.666
Take a moment with what you've just heard.
On page four, under What did you

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:41.913
notice, write a word, a phrase or a
question. What stayed with you? What

00:07:41.914 --> 00:07:46.759
would you like to understand more deeply?
Stay close to what the speakers actually

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.604
said. If you're watching in a live group,
you can share a brief observation in the

00:07:51.605 --> 00:07:56.091
chat if you wish. You're equally welcome
to keep it in your notes. Pause for

00:07:56.092 --> 00:07:57.945
longer here if that would help.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:11.243
As you look at your note, check whether it
is something a speaker told us, or an

00:08:11.244 --> 00:08:15.725
interpretation you've added. That
distinction matters. We can take a

00:08:15.726 --> 00:08:21.143
question forward without turning it into a
conclusion about every Autistic person.

00:08:21.144 --> 00:08:25.557
The sensory and thinking ideas that follow
offer ways to ask better

00:08:25.558 --> 00:08:30.440
questions. In our next session, we'll also
return to communication and the

00:08:30.441 --> 00:08:31.176
school day.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:37.449
A neurodiversity-affirming approach begins
with the person. People know, do,

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:41.837
learn and connect in different ways.
Differences are not automatically

00:08:41.838 --> 00:08:46.671
deficits, and there is no single correct
way for a mind to work. Everyone has

00:08:46.672 --> 00:08:51.250
strengths and experiences challenges. So
we need room in our thinking for

00:08:51.251 --> 00:08:56.210
interests, joy and connection as well as
difficulty. Being strengths based does

00:08:56.211 --> 00:09:00.789
not mean overlooking distress or
substantial support needs. It means that

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.941
those needs are understood within a whole
person's life. Consider the students you

00:09:05.942 --> 00:09:11.029
have in mind. What becomes visible when
you include what they enjoy and what they

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:16.053
do well? And what disappears if the only
thing we describe is behaviour we would

00:09:16.054 --> 00:09:20.886
like to change? We can say, this part of
the routine asks a great deal of the

00:09:20.887 --> 00:09:26.038
student, and investigate the support they
need. That opens more possibilities than

00:09:26.039 --> 00:09:31.126
deciding the student is simply unwilling.
Our job is to stay curious about access

00:09:31.127 --> 00:09:31.889
and support.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:38.202
The social model helps us look for
barriers in places, tasks, routines and

00:09:38.203 --> 00:09:43.203
expectations. Before asking a student to
change, ask what could change around

00:09:43.204 --> 00:09:47.809
them. Think about waiting in a noisy lunch
line. There might be several

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:52.876
demands at once: voices close by, an
uncertain wait, people brushing past, and

00:09:52.877 --> 00:09:57.482
spoken instructions competing with the
noise. Those are features we can

00:09:57.483 --> 00:10:02.680
investigate. We might ask whether there is
a clearer sequence, a different place

00:10:02.681 --> 00:10:07.484
to wait, or a choice that would make
access more workable. Those ideas are

00:10:07.485 --> 00:10:12.090
possibilities to explore with the student,
rather than solutions we can

00:10:12.091 --> 00:10:16.827
assume. Their communication and experience
help us judge whether a change

00:10:16.828 --> 00:10:22.157
helps. Changing the environment will not
make every difficulty disappear. It gives

00:10:22.158 --> 00:10:26.433
us an important part of the picture that
we can act on and review.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:33.018
We also need a picture of support that can
change. Dynamic disability means that

00:10:33.019 --> 00:10:38.012
access and support needs can vary with the
task, the environment, relationships

00:10:38.013 --> 00:10:43.006
and available capacity. A student may
manage a routine on one day and need much

00:10:43.007 --> 00:10:47.745
more support with it on another. The
routine may look the same to us, while

00:10:47.746 --> 00:10:52.675
the demands around it or the resources
available to the student are different.

00:10:52.676 --> 00:10:57.222
What they managed yesterday is useful
information; it is not a reason to

00:10:57.223 --> 00:11:02.344
dismiss what they need today. Strengths
and substantial support needs can both be

00:11:02.345 --> 00:11:07.467
present. Instead of treating ability as a
fixed amount, we can ask: what is being

00:11:07.468 --> 00:11:12.269
asked here, what support is available, and
what is the student communicating

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:16.302
now? This is a reason to keep noticing
across settings and days.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:23.083
Many of us learnt five senses at school:
touch, vision, taste, hearing and smell.

00:11:23.084 --> 00:11:27.671
This slide adds three that are useful for
understanding the school day.

00:11:27.672 --> 00:11:32.981
Proprioception is our sense of where our
body is in space. It includes information

00:11:32.982 --> 00:11:37.766
from muscles and joints. Think about
knowing how much pressure to use when

00:11:37.767 --> 00:11:42.485
holding a pencil or where your feet are as
you move around furniture. The

00:11:42.486 --> 00:11:47.336
vestibular sense relates to balance and
movement. It helps us make sense of

00:11:47.337 --> 00:11:52.514
movement and the position of the head.
Experiences of stairs, climbing, swinging

00:11:52.515 --> 00:11:57.234
or sitting still can differ from person to
person. Interoception concerns

00:11:57.235 --> 00:12:02.412
internal body signals. We'll come back to
that shortly, because noticing hunger,

00:12:02.413 --> 00:12:07.590
thirst or a need for the bathroom is part
of daily access too. These senses work

00:12:07.591 --> 00:12:12.179
together. A classroom is not just
something a student sees. It also has

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:17.292
sound, movement, textures, smells and
bodily demands. We need to find out how a

00:12:17.293 --> 00:12:21.880
particular student experiences those
things, including what they enjoy.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:28.447
Sensory experience includes pleasure and
connection, as well as discomfort or

00:12:28.448 --> 00:12:33.694
overload. Notice sensory joy. That might
be an experience a student seeks out or

00:12:33.695 --> 00:12:38.941
returns to because it feels good or helps
them regulate. Stimming can be part of

00:12:38.942 --> 00:12:43.590
regulation, pleasure and authentic
expression. We should not discourage

00:12:43.591 --> 00:12:49.037
harmless stimming simply because it looks
different. Nor should we treat difference

00:12:49.038 --> 00:12:54.151
as disruption by default. Bring one of
your students to mind. Where do you see

00:12:54.152 --> 00:12:59.132
enjoyment, ease or a way of expressing
themselves that matters to them? Keep

00:12:59.133 --> 00:13:03.914
that alongside anything you find difficult
to understand. The most useful

00:13:03.915 --> 00:13:09.095
question may be: what would this student
like us to know about this experience?

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:15.943
The next image builds a whole picture from
its details. Detail-first processing

00:13:15.944 --> 00:13:21.151
means taking in details, then building
understanding from those pieces. It is

00:13:21.152 --> 00:13:26.495
sometimes called bottom-up processing.
This is a useful lens on experience, not

00:13:26.496 --> 00:13:31.839
a test that tells us how every Autistic
student thinks. The guide invites us to

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:36.430
notice strengths such as pattern
recognition, systems thinking, deep

00:13:36.431 --> 00:13:41.775
memory and logical precision. It also asks
us to recognise honesty, directness,

00:13:41.776 --> 00:13:46.571
depth of connection and intense
engagement. Those strengths matter now;

00:13:46.572 --> 00:13:51.778
they aren't a consolation prize for
something else a student finds hard. Turn

00:13:51.779 --> 00:13:57.123
to Strengths I can see on page five. For
each student, note one strength you've

00:13:57.124 --> 00:14:02.330
actually observed and where it appears.
Perhaps they notice an inconsistency,

00:14:02.331 --> 00:14:07.263
remember something important, or
understand a topic in unusual depth. Use

00:14:07.264 --> 00:14:10.552
your own evidence. Pause if you'd like
more time.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.196
A specific description gives us something
we can build on. They remember the

00:14:23.197 --> 00:14:27.833
sequence of events in detail tells us more
than a general label. It can also

00:14:27.834 --> 00:14:32.408
change how peers and adults see a
student's contribution. At the same time,

00:14:32.409 --> 00:14:37.168
noticing more detail may come with more to
process. Strength and effort can be

00:14:37.169 --> 00:14:38.899
part of the same experience.

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:45.017
Monotropism is a framework for
understanding attention going deeply into

00:14:45.018 --> 00:14:49.896
fewer things at a time. The torch and
floodlight on the slide offer a way to

00:14:49.897 --> 00:14:54.774
picture different distributions of
attention. Neither is a better way to be.

00:14:54.775 --> 00:14:59.913
A deep interest can provide engagement,
learning, memory and joy. When a student

00:14:59.914 --> 00:15:04.596
is absorbed in a construction task, a
drawing or a topic they love, their

00:15:04.597 --> 00:15:09.539
attention may be strongly invested there.
Asking them to stop and pack up for

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:14.483
recess can involve much more than putting
down a pencil. The idea of tendrils

00:15:14.484 --> 00:15:19.296
offers another image: attention has
reached into the activity, and shifting

00:15:19.297 --> 00:15:24.305
means drawing those connections back. A
warning, a clear next step or a way to

00:15:24.306 --> 00:15:29.183
return to unfinished work may help,
depending on the student. We can protect

00:15:29.184 --> 00:15:34.452
moments of flow while making transitions
more accessible. The question becomes how

00:15:34.453 --> 00:15:38.680
to support a shift, rather than how to
make an interest disappear.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:45.831
Executive functioning gives us language
for the demands involved in doing things.

00:15:45.832 --> 00:15:50.899
Planning concerns the steps ahead.
Organisation helps us manage materials

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:56.320
and information. Time management includes
noticing time and working within it.

00:15:56.321 --> 00:16:01.670
Working memory helps us hold information
while using it. Meta-cognition means

00:16:01.671 --> 00:16:06.528
thinking about our own thinking. Emotional
regulation, flexibility and

00:16:06.529 --> 00:16:11.315
attention affect how we respond and shift.
Task initiation is getting

00:16:11.316 --> 00:16:16.735
started, and persistence is continuing
with a task. These terms are lenses for

00:16:16.736 --> 00:16:22.015
inquiry, not a checklist for labelling a
student. Take an ordinary classroom

00:16:22.016 --> 00:16:27.506
instruction: finish this, put it away,
collect something else and join the next

00:16:27.507 --> 00:16:32.645
activity. How much must a student
remember, sequence, organise and switch?

00:16:32.646 --> 00:16:38.347
Useful support responds to the particular
demand. We'll return to concrete support

00:16:38.348 --> 00:16:40.107
options in Session Three.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:46.411
Let's return to interoception, the sense
of internal body signals. Those signals

00:16:46.412 --> 00:16:50.881
help us make sense of temperature, hunger,
thirst and needing the bathroom.

00:16:50.882 --> 00:16:55.472
They can be part of understanding how
we're doing during the day. Alexithymia

00:16:55.473 --> 00:16:59.519
means difficulty identifying and
describing emotions and feelings. A

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.808
person might find it hard to work out
whether a feeling is excitement or

00:17:03.809 --> 00:17:08.701
anxiety, or to put a feeling into words.
Difficulty naming a feeling does not mean

00:17:08.702 --> 00:17:13.594
there is no feeling. The questions on the
slide remind us that internal experience

00:17:13.595 --> 00:17:18.366
may not be obvious from outside. Crying
does not tell us the whole story, and an

00:17:18.367 --> 00:17:23.198
apparently quiet student may still need
support. We can offer ways to communicate

00:17:23.199 --> 00:17:27.850
and remain curious, without claiming to
know someone's internal state from one

00:17:27.851 --> 00:17:28.635
outward sign.

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:34.680
Now let's apply this to one familiar
classroom routine. Open the Sensory

00:17:34.681 --> 00:17:39.774
activity on page five. You might choose
lining up, mat time, visiting the library

00:17:39.775 --> 00:17:44.550
or eating lunch. Choose something you know
well, without naming a student or

00:17:44.551 --> 00:17:49.517
school. First, name the routine. Then list
what the environment or task asks of

00:17:49.518 --> 00:17:53.975
students before listing any student
responses. Consider the demands you

00:17:53.976 --> 00:17:58.942
actually notice in your setting. Next, add
possible options that could increase

00:17:58.943 --> 00:18:03.718
access or choice, including a way for
students to communicate what helps. In

00:18:03.719 --> 00:18:08.557
the final column, write a question to
explore. If you think a different place

00:18:08.558 --> 00:18:13.715
to sit might help, what would you need to
ask or observe before assuming that? You

00:18:13.716 --> 00:18:18.491
can work privately. If colleagues are
watching together, you may pause for a

00:18:18.492 --> 00:18:23.394
short conversation and compare options.
One grounded question is enough. Pause

00:18:23.395 --> 00:18:28.489
the video for about five minutes, or as
long as you need, and then return for the

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:28.998
debrief.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:41.838
Look back at the order of your notes. Did
you begin with environmental and task

00:18:41.839 --> 00:18:46.677
demands? If you started with a student's
response, see whether you can move one

00:18:46.678 --> 00:18:51.330
step earlier and describe what the routine
asks. Now check the option you've

00:18:51.331 --> 00:18:55.983
suggested. Does it offer access or choice?
How could a student indicate that

00:18:55.984 --> 00:19:00.760
it helps, that it doesn't, or that they
would prefer something else? Different

00:19:00.761 --> 00:19:05.103
students may need different options within
the same routine. We haven't

00:19:05.104 --> 00:19:10.067
simulated what it is like to be Autistic.
We've examined a routine that educators

00:19:10.068 --> 00:19:14.906
can change, and identified what we still
need to understand. That's the inquiry

00:19:14.907 --> 00:19:16.890
we'll continue between sessions.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.701
Before Session Two, there is a light,
optional noticing task on page six. You

00:19:23.702 --> 00:19:29.282
do not need to create an extra activity
for students. Notice one familiar routine

00:19:29.283 --> 00:19:34.724
you are already part of. What supports
access, connection or joy? What seems to

00:19:34.725 --> 00:19:40.375
add demand, and when? What don't you know
yet? Write that last part as a question.

00:19:40.376 --> 00:19:46.026
Keep the notes anonymous, and bring them
to the next session. If you don't have an

00:19:46.027 --> 00:19:51.467
opportunity to do this, you can still take
part fully. Session Two will explore

00:19:51.468 --> 00:19:56.490
communication, connection and the way
demands and supports build across a

00:19:56.491 --> 00:20:01.862
primary school day. Thank you for making
space to look more closely. Keep your

00:20:01.863 --> 00:20:04.513
students, and your questions, in view.

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:17.855
Welcome back to Primary Education Online.
In this second session, we're looking at

00:20:17.855 --> 00:20:22.992
communication, connection and capacity
across a primary school day. We'll build

00:20:22.992 --> 00:20:27.800
a simple map of that day and choose a
focus for our design work next time.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:32.674
Bring back the two or three students
you've been holding in mind. If you're

00:20:32.674 --> 00:20:37.811
joining here, you can choose them now.
Keep your notes anonymous. The key ideas

00:20:37.811 --> 00:20:43.081
for this session begin on page seven of
the Participant Guide. Before we move on,

00:20:43.081 --> 00:20:47.954
take a moment with anything you noticed
since Session One. What helped in a

00:20:47.954 --> 00:20:53.289
familiar routine? What added demand? What
question stayed open? If you didn't have

00:20:53.289 --> 00:20:58.493
a chance to do the noticing task, that's
fine. Think about a routine you already

00:20:58.493 --> 00:21:03.366
know. Private reflection is enough. If
you're watching with a live group, a

00:21:03.366 --> 00:21:08.635
short observation in the chat is welcome,
with no identifying details. Pause here

00:21:08.635 --> 00:21:11.730
if you'd like time to reconnect with your
notes.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:25.562
Keep that observation beside you as we go.
We'll look beyond the timetable to

00:21:25.563 --> 00:21:31.823
how communication, connection and demands
shape the day. As before, you can listen,

00:21:31.824 --> 00:21:37.703
write, pass, or step away. In a live
group, cameras remain optional. Take part

00:21:37.704 --> 00:21:40.146
in the way that helps you think.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:46.287
Let's start with a quick question. Since
you woke up today, how have you

00:21:46.288 --> 00:21:51.025
communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. You might have

00:21:51.026 --> 00:21:56.282
spoken, sent a message, pointed, shown a
photo, moved closer to someone or taken a

00:21:56.283 --> 00:22:01.474
moment before responding. Choose one and
note it privately, or put it in the chat

00:22:01.475 --> 00:22:06.537
if you're watching with colleagues. I'll
leave a short pause. You can pause the

00:22:06.538 --> 00:22:08.289
video longer if you'd like.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:21.407
Even in an online session, people may be
communicating through words, reactions,

00:22:21.408 --> 00:22:26.687
facial expression, or a choice about when
to speak. Communication takes many forms,

00:22:26.688 --> 00:22:31.260
and speech is one part of it. At school, a
request to use your words can

00:22:31.261 --> 00:22:36.412
accidentally close off a way a student is
already communicating. Pointing, signs,

00:22:36.413 --> 00:22:41.435
writing or a communication device are
valid ways to share a message too. We can

00:22:41.436 --> 00:22:46.458
notice the message and make sure there is
a workable way for it to be received.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:52.757
Understanding and expressing are
connected, but they aren't the same

00:22:52.758 --> 00:22:58.271
thing. Receptive communication concerns
information coming in and how a person

00:22:58.272 --> 00:23:02.998
makes sense of it. That might include
spoken words, writing, visual

00:23:02.999 --> 00:23:08.583
information, demonstration or gesture.
Expressive communication is how a person

00:23:08.584 --> 00:23:13.883
sends a message out. It may involve
speech, writing, typing, movement, sign

00:23:13.884 --> 00:23:19.612
or AAC. AAC stands for augmentative and
alternative communication. It can include

00:23:19.613 --> 00:23:24.983
a communication board or book, signs,
typing or a device. Some people use it

00:23:24.984 --> 00:23:30.640
alongside speech and some use it instead
of speech. How much someone speaks does

00:23:30.641 --> 00:23:36.154
not tell us how much they understand.
Fluent speech doesn't guarantee that all

00:23:36.155 --> 00:23:41.239
spoken instructions were understood.
Limited speech doesn't mean limited

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:46.681
understanding. Communication can also
change with the person, the setting and

00:23:46.682 --> 00:23:52.052
the demands of the moment. Think about one
task in your classroom. How can a

00:23:52.053 --> 00:23:57.638
student receive the information, and how
can they respond? Those are two access

00:23:57.639 --> 00:24:00.359
questions, and both deserve attention.

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:06.407
We're going to listen to Autistic people
describe communication experiences. As

00:24:06.408 --> 00:24:10.238
you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

00:24:10.239 --> 00:24:14.650
stands out to you. Pay attention to the
communication methods available, what

00:24:14.651 --> 00:24:18.713
another person does, and the part the
environment plays. We'll play the

00:24:18.714 --> 00:24:19.990
original film in full.

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:56.980
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

00:29:56.981 --> 00:30:01.420
name one specific thing from the film. You
might consider a communication

00:30:01.421 --> 00:30:06.106
strength, something a communication
partner did, or the difference made by an

00:30:06.107 --> 00:30:10.669
available method. Keep the speakers' own
accounts at the centre. Write your

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:15.356
thought in your notes, or share a short
phrase if you're watching with a live

00:30:15.357 --> 00:30:18.439
group. Pause for a little longer if you
would like.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:31.910
Keep your observation as a question for
your own setting. There is no single

00:30:31.911 --> 00:30:37.454
Autistic communication profile. What
helped one speaker gives us something to

00:30:37.455 --> 00:30:42.851
consider, rather than a method to
prescribe for everyone. For a student who

00:30:42.852 --> 00:30:48.031
uses AAC, access needs to be there where
communication happens: during a

00:30:48.032 --> 00:30:52.772
transition, out in the playground, or when
a choice is being made.

00:30:52.773 --> 00:30:57.878
Communication is part of the whole day,
not only a particular activity.

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:04.460
There are three useful starting points on
this slide. Clear communication means

00:31:04.461 --> 00:31:09.261
using familiar words and offering one idea
or step at a time. It also means

00:31:09.262 --> 00:31:14.063
being specific enough that the person
doesn't have to guess what we intend.

00:31:14.064 --> 00:31:18.670
Processing time means giving time to
understand, organise a response and

00:31:18.671 --> 00:31:23.731
communicate it. A silence can feel long
online, or in a busy classroom. Filling

00:31:23.732 --> 00:31:28.467
it with a second question or a new version
of the instruction may add more

00:31:28.468 --> 00:31:33.204
information to process. We can practise
letting the pause be there. Visual

00:31:33.205 --> 00:31:38.135
supports mean showing information as well
as saying it. A photograph, written

00:31:38.136 --> 00:31:43.196
instruction, object or sequence may help,
where the student understands and can

00:31:43.197 --> 00:31:47.933
access it. These are options to match to
the person and moment. Keep their

00:31:47.934 --> 00:31:52.864
preferred ways to respond available,
including a way to refuse, ask for help,

00:31:52.865 --> 00:31:54.616
or say they need more time.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:03.340
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

00:32:03.341 --> 00:32:08.439
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, understanding one

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:13.802
another can be harder for both people. If
an interaction goes wrong, we should not

00:32:13.803 --> 00:32:18.437
automatically locate the whole problem
within the Autistic student. The

00:32:18.438 --> 00:32:23.404
expectations of adults and peers also
shape what happens. Whose expectations

00:32:23.405 --> 00:32:28.370
are operating during group work, a
classroom discussion or a conversation in

00:32:28.371 --> 00:32:33.402
the playground? Are those expectations
clear, and do they allow more than one

00:32:33.403 --> 00:32:38.435
way to connect? A student's preferred
style might be direct, brief, detailed,

00:32:38.436 --> 00:32:43.401
quiet, or based around a shared interest.
We can ask what makes the exchange

00:32:43.402 --> 00:32:47.837
clearer and safer for everyone. Connection
does not need to look one

00:32:47.838 --> 00:32:49.956
particular way to be meaningful.

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:56.010
The picture of connection reaches beyond
the classroom. Students may connect with

00:32:56.011 --> 00:33:00.597
family, at school, in their community or
online. Culture and individual experience

00:33:00.598 --> 00:33:05.071
shape what connection means and how it is
expressed. Some students enjoy talking

00:33:05.072 --> 00:33:09.658
one to one. Others enjoy making something
alongside another person, sharing a deep

00:33:09.659 --> 00:33:13.792
interest, playing, or simply being
together quietly. Frequent conversation

00:33:13.793 --> 00:33:18.153
is only one possible form of connection.
We don't need private details about a

00:33:18.154 --> 00:33:22.230
student's online life or community
membership to keep our thinking broad.

00:33:22.231 --> 00:33:26.647
Let's bring this back to the kinds of
connection you actually notice at school,

00:33:26.648 --> 00:33:29.931
and to what the students themselves
communicate about them.

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:36.147
Open the Connection discussion on page
eight. Think about the students you have

00:33:36.148 --> 00:33:40.371
in mind, and take these questions one at a
time. When do they seem most

00:33:40.372 --> 00:33:44.838
comfortable connecting with others at
school? What kinds of connection does

00:33:44.839 --> 00:33:49.666
each of them enjoy? It might be talking,
gaming, sport, making things, sharing an

00:33:49.667 --> 00:33:53.891
interest or being quietly alongside
someone. Are there times when being

00:33:53.892 --> 00:33:58.779
social seems enjoyable, and times when it
seems tiring or overwhelming? What could

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:03.668
educators change to make connection feel
safer, easier or more meaningful? You can

00:34:03.669 --> 00:34:07.953
jot a few private notes. If you're
watching with colleagues, pause for a

00:34:07.954 --> 00:34:12.238
short conversation about the conditions
for connection, keeping students

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:17.067
anonymous. You don't need to work through
every example. Choose one question that

00:34:17.068 --> 00:34:20.386
helps you look again, and pause for as
long as you need.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:33.179
Look at the last part of your response:
what could educators change? That keeps

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:37.530
us focused on something we can investigate
or offer. If a student enjoys

00:34:37.531 --> 00:34:42.126
working alongside someone but doesn't want
a conversation, can that count as

00:34:42.127 --> 00:34:46.722
connection? If an activity is enjoyable at
one time of day and exhausting at

00:34:46.723 --> 00:34:51.318
another, what else is happening around it?
We can offer options and ask what

00:34:51.319 --> 00:34:56.282
works, rather than decide that one social
style is the standard every student must

00:34:56.283 --> 00:34:56.588
meet.

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:03.929
The battery image gives us a way to talk
about energy and capacity. A day does not

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:09.093
always begin with the same charge.
Activities can use resources, and what

00:35:09.094 --> 00:35:14.617
happens across the day can affect what is
available later. Some people use the

00:35:14.618 --> 00:35:19.853
language of spoons: having a limited
number available, using some for each

00:35:19.854 --> 00:35:25.448
action, and finding things harder when
spoons are low. These are metaphors that

00:35:25.449 --> 00:35:30.971
help us ask about experience, not a way to
measure a student's internal state.

00:35:30.972 --> 00:35:36.565
The picture also includes recharging.
Support, recovery, familiar connection or

00:35:36.566 --> 00:35:42.089
something enjoyable may restore some
capacity. Something can be worthwhile and

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:47.755
still take effort. Keep both the cost and
what lightens it in mind as we map the

00:35:47.756 --> 00:35:48.041
day.

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:54.948
Turn to page nine, Map a primary school
day. Start with arrival and finish with

00:35:54.949 --> 00:36:00.405
departure. Add the moments that matter in
your classroom: routines, transitions,

00:36:00.406 --> 00:36:05.932
changes and opportunities to communicate.
Mark where there is a chance to choose,

00:36:05.933 --> 00:36:11.252
pause or recover. Then consider how the
same moments might land for Student A,

00:36:11.253 --> 00:36:16.571
Student B and Student C. Use what you know
from observation and what they have

00:36:16.572 --> 00:36:22.029
communicated. Leave a question where you
don't know. The guide offers letters if

00:36:22.030 --> 00:36:27.279
they help: R for a routine or transition,
C for change, T for a communication

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:32.530
opportunity and P for a pause, choice or
recovery point. Follow the students'

00:36:32.531 --> 00:36:37.504
experience, not only the official
timetable. Waiting, finding information

00:36:37.505 --> 00:36:43.031
or moving to the next activity may matter
as much as the named lesson. If a whole

00:36:43.032 --> 00:36:48.696
day feels too much, begin with arrival to
recess and extend it later. This map is a

00:36:48.697 --> 00:36:53.877
working picture, not a judgement of a
student or school. Pause the video for

00:36:53.878 --> 00:36:59.404
about five to seven minutes to sketch it,
or spend less time and add to it later.

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:12.824
Keep the map open. It doesn't need to be
complete to be useful. A few moments can

00:37:12.825 --> 00:37:17.805
already show you what the day asks and
where information is missing. Notice

00:37:17.806 --> 00:37:23.055
whether you have included opportunities
for communication and recovery, as well

00:37:23.056 --> 00:37:28.305
as the activities students are expected to
complete. We're going to add another

00:37:28.306 --> 00:37:30.055
layer to the same map now.

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:36.364
Page ten asks, what costs and what
lightens? Return to your map. With one

00:37:36.365 --> 00:37:41.703
mark or colour, identify things that may
add demand or uncertainty. With another,

00:37:41.704 --> 00:37:46.441
identify what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Perhaps a

00:37:46.442 --> 00:37:51.379
predictable start is helpful, or a
familiar person makes it easier to ask a

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:56.451
question. Perhaps a transition carries
more uncertainty than adults realised.

00:37:56.452 --> 00:38:01.456
Use examples from your setting and keep
them open to checking with students.

00:38:01.457 --> 00:38:06.795
Where is a student's experience unknown?
Add a question there. Enjoying something

00:38:06.796 --> 00:38:11.867
does not mean it costs nothing, and
completing something does not tell us how

00:38:11.868 --> 00:38:17.339
it felt. Take a few minutes to extend the
map. If you are watching with colleagues,

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:21.744
you can optionally share a general pattern
under costs, lightens or

00:38:21.745 --> 00:38:26.682
questions. Keep the individual map
private. Pause now if you'd like longer.

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:40.087
As you look across the map, include what
lightens the day as carefully as what

00:38:40.088 --> 00:38:45.595
makes it harder. Strengths, interests,
safe connection and belonging are part of

00:38:45.596 --> 00:38:50.824
the picture. This isn't a score. We aren't
assigning a battery percentage to

00:38:50.825 --> 00:38:56.193
a child. We are making visible what we
know, what we suspect, and what we need

00:38:56.194 --> 00:39:01.282
to ask. That distinction will help us
choose a useful design question next

00:39:01.283 --> 00:39:01.840
session.

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:08.638
The school-day stack shows how demands can
accumulate. The darker bars represent

00:39:08.639 --> 00:39:13.784
demand; the lighter bars represent relief
and recovery. It is an illustration,

00:39:13.785 --> 00:39:18.996
rather than a scale for rating students. A
moment that seems small in isolation

00:39:18.997 --> 00:39:24.075
may follow several earlier demands. A
change near the end of the day may land

00:39:24.076 --> 00:39:29.488
differently from the same change after a
period of recovery. Look back at your own

00:39:29.489 --> 00:39:34.166
map. Where are the demands close together?
Where are support and relief

00:39:34.167 --> 00:39:39.579
available? We're widening our view beyond
the last thing that happened. We can ask

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:44.590
what preceded a difficult moment, and what
could make the pattern of the day

00:39:44.591 --> 00:39:45.526
more workable.

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:51.697
Outward behaviour does not show everything
a student is managing. Masking

00:39:51.698 --> 00:39:56.550
can involve working to hide or manage
parts of oneself in order to appear to

00:39:56.551 --> 00:40:01.532
fit the expectations around us. That
effort may be missed when we look only at

00:40:01.533 --> 00:40:06.060
quiet behaviour or completed work. This
topic can connect with personal

00:40:06.061 --> 00:40:11.042
experience. You are welcome to step away
or simply listen. There is no need to

00:40:11.043 --> 00:40:16.024
share a personal story. As educators, we
can ask what a student may be working

00:40:16.025 --> 00:40:20.682
hard to manage, where they feel
recognised, and what belonging would look

00:40:20.683 --> 00:40:25.341
like without a requirement to appear less
Autistic. We must keep those as

00:40:25.342 --> 00:40:30.128
questions, rather than decide from
appearance alone that we know someone is

00:40:30.129 --> 00:40:30.646
masking.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:36.770
On page eleven, the Masking discussion
begins with two private questions. When

00:40:36.771 --> 00:40:41.442
might one of the students you have in mind
be working hard to appear fine? When

00:40:41.443 --> 00:40:46.173
and where do they seem most comfortable
being themselves? Keep your responses to

00:40:46.174 --> 00:40:50.904
those questions in your own notes. Then
consider the third: what could we change

00:40:50.905 --> 00:40:55.575
to help them feel more comfortable and
supported? If you are watching in a live

00:40:55.576 --> 00:41:00.186
group, you may share a general idea about
what could change. You don't need to

00:41:00.187 --> 00:41:04.438
disclose a student's experience or a
sensitive incident. Pause for a few

00:41:04.439 --> 00:41:07.731
minutes of reflection, then come back when
you're ready.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:20.709
Check that any change you have written
allows authentic participation. The aim

00:41:20.710 --> 00:41:25.659
is not to get better at identifying who
looks fine, or to require a student to

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:30.930
disclose something private. It is to make
room for communication and support before

00:41:30.931 --> 00:41:35.751
someone has to reach a point of distress.
The student's own account, and the

00:41:35.752 --> 00:41:40.187
knowledge held across home and school, may
add things we haven't seen.

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:47.371
Let's carry a clear focus into design.
Open page twelve. Under Keep, choose one

00:41:47.372 --> 00:41:52.805
rhythm worth protecting because it
supports access, connection or joy. Under

00:41:52.806 --> 00:41:58.457
Reduce, choose one barrier or unnecessary
demand. Tick whether it sits within a

00:41:58.458 --> 00:42:03.674
rotation, a transition or a whole-class
routine. Under Explore, write one

00:42:03.675 --> 00:42:08.891
question to take to students, families or
the team. The question can stay

00:42:08.892 --> 00:42:14.543
unanswered today. It may be what makes
your next step more thoughtful. Keep the

00:42:14.544 --> 00:42:19.905
focus small enough to work with. One
routine is plenty. Pause to make those

00:42:19.906 --> 00:42:24.397
notes now, and keep your day map beside
them for Session Three.

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:36.915
You now have a starting point for a
practical change. Keep reminds us to

00:42:36.916 --> 00:42:42.067
protect what already helps. Reduce points
to something we may be able to redesign.

00:42:42.068 --> 00:42:46.583
Explore keeps consultation in the process.
Next session, we'll use those

00:42:46.584 --> 00:42:51.099
notes to write a brief and test a possible
change against your different

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:51.671
students.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:57.911
Across these first two sessions, we've
been building a fuller picture of the

00:42:57.912 --> 00:43:02.686
students you have in mind. Their sensory
experiences, their ways of thinking,

00:43:02.687 --> 00:43:07.272
their communication and connection all
belong in that picture. So do their

00:43:07.273 --> 00:43:12.235
strengths, interests and the effort the
school day can require. The line on this

00:43:12.236 --> 00:43:17.198
slide is worth sitting with: a child who
knows who they are, and feels genuinely

00:43:17.199 --> 00:43:22.098
valued for it, has more available for
everything else. Belonging includes being

00:43:22.099 --> 00:43:26.182
able to participate as oneself. Agency
includes real choices about

00:43:26.183 --> 00:43:31.019
communication, support and when to step
back. A positive Autistic identity can

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:35.983
include recognising one's own experiences
and knowing that other Autistic people

00:43:35.984 --> 00:43:40.946
share aspects of them. No two students
need to express that identity in the same

00:43:40.947 --> 00:43:45.846
way. Keep supporting students to know
themselves, and to know that who they are

00:43:45.847 --> 00:43:50.621
is worth knowing. Thank you for your
attention today. Bring your map and your

00:43:50.622 --> 00:43:53.385
Keep, Reduce, Explore notes to Session
Three.

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:07.970
Welcome to Session Three, Designing for
Access in the Primary Classroom. Today

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:13.541
we'll use what you've noticed to make one
small, testable change to a familiar

00:44:13.541 --> 00:44:19.400
routine. Have your Participant Guide, day
map and Carry into design notes ready if

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:25.043
you can. Page thirteen summarises this
session. Your Reduce item from last time

00:44:25.043 --> 00:44:30.469
is a useful starting point: one barrier or
unnecessary demand in a rotation,

00:44:30.469 --> 00:44:36.184
transition or whole-class routine. If you
missed Session Two, you can still join

00:44:36.184 --> 00:44:41.609
in. Think of a familiar part of a school
day and make a quick sketch of what

00:44:41.609 --> 00:44:47.469
happens. Choose one moment you would like
to understand or improve. You don't need

00:44:47.469 --> 00:44:52.895
a complete map to begin. As before,
private notes and listening are welcome.

00:44:52.895 --> 00:44:58.320
If you are watching in a live group,
cameras and contributions are optional.

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:03.384
Pause whenever you need more thinking
time. We'll keep the focus on the

00:45:03.384 --> 00:45:08.809
environment, task and routine, with
students' experience guiding the design.

00:45:10.320 --> 00:45:15.028
Students come first. We are moving from
understanding to design without turning

00:45:15.029 --> 00:45:19.615
students into a list of problems. A
strategy becomes useful when it answers a

00:45:19.616 --> 00:45:24.324
particular access question. Separate an
observation from an interpretation. The

00:45:24.325 --> 00:45:29.032
instruction was given while materials were
being handed out describes something

00:45:29.033 --> 00:45:33.620
we can examine. The student didn't care is
an interpretation that closes down

00:45:33.621 --> 00:45:38.208
inquiry. A question such as, how could the
student return to the instruction,

00:45:38.209 --> 00:45:42.494
points us towards something we can change.
We'll design for one routine,

00:45:42.495 --> 00:45:47.444
then check the design against each of the
students you have in mind. What you don't

00:45:47.445 --> 00:45:51.549
yet know stays a question for
consultation. You don't need a complete

00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:55.412
explanation of a student before you can
begin removing a barrier.

00:45:56.921 --> 00:46:01.705
Keep a concise picture of each student
beside the design. What are their

00:46:01.706 --> 00:46:06.287
strengths, interests and sources of joy?
What do you know about their

00:46:06.288 --> 00:46:11.274
communication, sensory experience and
thinking? What costs them effort, and

00:46:11.275 --> 00:46:16.597
what lightens the day? Include identity
and belonging. Include knowledge already

00:46:16.598 --> 00:46:21.786
held by the student, family and team.
Leave room for open questions. You don't

00:46:21.787 --> 00:46:26.975
need to fill in a new profile or gather
private information for this exercise.

00:46:26.976 --> 00:46:32.097
The purpose of holding several students in
mind is to keep variety visible. A

00:46:32.098 --> 00:46:37.353
change that helps one person may make
little difference for another. We'll come

00:46:37.354 --> 00:46:40.990
back to that in the private check later in
the session.

00:46:42.521 --> 00:46:47.717
Open My brief on page fourteen. Choose one
routine you know well. It could be a

00:46:47.718 --> 00:46:52.848
rotation, a transition or a whole-class
routine, and it may be the Reduce item

00:46:52.849 --> 00:46:58.112
you brought from Session Two. Complete the
three sentence starters. This routine

00:46:58.113 --> 00:47:03.376
asks students to. For some students, that
means. We could change. For example, a

00:47:03.377 --> 00:47:08.307
routine might ask students to remember
several spoken steps while gathering

00:47:08.308 --> 00:47:13.371
materials. The access question could be
how they find the next step when they

00:47:13.372 --> 00:47:18.368
need it. That's an example to show the
shape of the brief, not an assumption

00:47:18.369 --> 00:47:23.432
about your students. Keep your wording
specific and within educator or school

00:47:23.433 --> 00:47:28.097
influence. Remove blame or deficit labels.
Where you aren't sure what a

00:47:28.098 --> 00:47:33.561
demand means for a student, write that as
a question. Pause for around five minutes

00:47:33.562 --> 00:47:38.425
to draft your brief. You can work
privately, or share only your general We

00:47:38.426 --> 00:47:41.822
could change line with colleagues if that
is useful.

00:47:49.841 --> 00:47:54.841
Read your brief back. Does it name what
the routine asks, rather than simply

00:47:54.842 --> 00:47:59.976
describe what you wish a student would do?
Is the possible change small enough

00:47:59.977 --> 00:48:05.444
to try? And are unknowns still visible as
questions? If your brief is broad, narrow

00:48:05.445 --> 00:48:10.845
it to one moment. You can always extend a
useful change later. Keep this one brief

00:48:10.846 --> 00:48:13.245
active while we look at the options.

00:48:14.761 --> 00:48:18.814
This menu offers four possible directions.
Executive functioning

00:48:18.815 --> 00:48:23.834
supports can answer a known task demand.
Predictability gives clear information

00:48:23.835 --> 00:48:28.790
about routine and change. Schedules make
sequence, timing and changes visible.

00:48:28.791 --> 00:48:33.552
Visual supports can help students find,
understand, remember or communicate

00:48:33.553 --> 00:48:38.572
information. Listen with your brief in
front of you. You are not being asked to

00:48:38.573 --> 00:48:43.527
add every option. Ask which one, or which
pair, might answer the demand you've

00:48:43.528 --> 00:48:48.675
described. There is space under Options I
chose on page fourteen to note the fit.

00:48:48.676 --> 00:48:52.987
We'll consider schedules alongside
predictability, because keeping a

00:48:52.988 --> 00:48:57.298
schedule accurate when plans change is
part of what makes it useful.

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:03.953
Executive functioning support should match
the demand. If getting started is

00:49:03.954 --> 00:49:09.243
difficult, making the first step visible
may be more useful than repeating the

00:49:09.244 --> 00:49:14.466
whole task. If the task asks students to
hold several instructions in mind, a

00:49:14.467 --> 00:49:19.757
sequence they can return to may reduce
that demand. Organisation might involve

00:49:19.758 --> 00:49:24.704
making materials easier to locate or
showing what is needed before a task

00:49:24.705 --> 00:49:29.858
starts. Time can be made clearer with
information about what will happen and

00:49:29.859 --> 00:49:34.874
when. A transition may be easier to
approach with preparation and a way to

00:49:34.875 --> 00:49:40.027
finish or return to the current work.
These are examples to consider, rather

00:49:40.028 --> 00:49:45.181
than a checklist to apply to every
student. Look at your brief. What exactly

00:49:45.182 --> 00:49:50.472
are you helping the student to do? How
will they be able to tell you, in a way

00:49:50.473 --> 00:49:55.282
that works for them, whether the support
helps? A support should reduce

00:49:55.283 --> 00:50:00.779
unnecessary demand. If it adds more adult
prompting or pressure without improving

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:02.840
access, we need to look again.

00:50:04.360 --> 00:50:09.273
Predictability means having useful
information about routine and change. It

00:50:09.274 --> 00:50:13.855
doesn't require every day to be identical,
and it isn't a promise that

00:50:13.856 --> 00:50:18.902
nothing unexpected will happen. Think
about how much information in a primary

00:50:18.903 --> 00:50:24.347
classroom is held by adults, spoken once,
or implied. Can students find out what is

00:50:24.348 --> 00:50:29.460
happening, what is changing, what comes
next, and what they can do if the plan

00:50:29.461 --> 00:50:34.374
changes? Give that information early
enough to be useful. A schedule is one

00:50:34.375 --> 00:50:39.620
way to make sequence and change visible.
The example on the slide is an assembly

00:50:39.621 --> 00:50:44.932
moving earlier. The class schedule needs
to change too. Move the card and explain

00:50:44.933 --> 00:50:49.979
the change, rather than leave students to
discover that the displayed plan is

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:54.362
wrong. Then ask whether students can
return to that information and

00:50:54.363 --> 00:50:59.077
communicate a question or need for
support. A schedule that is accurate,

00:50:59.078 --> 00:51:04.389
understood and available offers access. A
schedule that students cannot interpret

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:07.842
or that no longer reflects the day may add
confusion.

00:51:09.361 --> 00:51:14.571
A visual support should clarify something
the student needs to find, understand,

00:51:14.572 --> 00:51:19.782
remember or communicate. Begin with that
need, rather than with a tool you would

00:51:19.783 --> 00:51:23.938
like to use. Objects, photographs, symbols
and written words are

00:51:23.939 --> 00:51:28.159
possibilities. A visual is not
automatically accessible. Does the

00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:33.370
student understand what it represents? Can
they reach or find it at the point of

00:51:33.371 --> 00:51:38.515
use? Does it reflect what is actually
happening, including change? Does it give

00:51:38.516 --> 00:51:43.726
useful information or a way to respond?
Those quick-review questions are on page

00:51:43.727 --> 00:51:48.673
fifteen. If your design uses a visual
support, run through them. Explain how

00:51:48.674 --> 00:51:53.554
the support works and check its meaning
with the student. Avoid unnecessary

00:51:53.555 --> 00:51:58.831
detail. Remember the distinction between
understanding information and expressing

00:51:58.832 --> 00:52:03.383
a message. A schedule may support
understanding, while a student's AAC

00:52:03.384 --> 00:52:08.396
gives ways to communicate. Keep both forms
of access in view. The slides also

00:52:08.397 --> 00:52:13.673
name a separate Visual Supports Checklist
and a webinar. Their access details are

00:52:13.674 --> 00:52:18.752
still to be confirmed in the supplied
materials. The quick-review questions in

00:52:18.753 --> 00:52:21.851
your Participant Guide are available to
use now.

00:52:23.361 --> 00:52:28.078
Universal Design for Learning gives us
another way to examine the emerging

00:52:28.079 --> 00:52:33.183
design. Is important information clear and
available? Are there workable ways to

00:52:33.184 --> 00:52:37.836
participate and respond? Does the design
support engagement while keeping

00:52:37.837 --> 00:52:42.812
meaningful challenge? For example, a
learning goal can remain worthwhile while

00:52:42.813 --> 00:52:47.917
students have more than one way to show
what they know. The information students

00:52:47.918 --> 00:52:52.570
need can be available in a form they can
return to, rather than depend on

00:52:52.571 --> 00:52:57.482
remembering one spoken explanation.
Individual knowledge and universal design

00:52:57.483 --> 00:53:02.522
can inform each other. What we learn from
a particular student's experience may

00:53:02.523 --> 00:53:07.434
reveal a barrier affecting others too.
Making an option available to everyone

00:53:07.435 --> 00:53:12.539
can improve access, while some students
will still need something more specific.

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:16.998
The presence of a resource alone does not
tell us access has improved.

00:53:18.521 --> 00:53:24.184
Let's check your draft. On page fourteen,
choose one or two options that seem to

00:53:24.185 --> 00:53:29.847
answer your brief and note why they fit.
Then turn to the Accessibility check on

00:53:29.848 --> 00:53:35.224
page fifteen. Can students find and
understand what they need? Can they take

00:53:35.225 --> 00:53:40.458
part in more than one way? Can they pause,
ask for help or recover? Is the

00:53:40.459 --> 00:53:45.835
support available when demand increases?
If an answer is not yet, adjust the

00:53:45.836 --> 00:53:51.642
design. Perhaps information is available
at the start but disappears when the task

00:53:51.643 --> 00:53:57.090
begins. Perhaps a student can see what to
do but has no workable way to ask a

00:53:57.091 --> 00:54:02.610
question. You don't need a perfect design
on paper. Make the next version more

00:54:02.611 --> 00:54:08.274
useful, and keep the questions you will
test through experience. Pause for a few

00:54:08.275 --> 00:54:11.786
minutes to work through the check, then
come back.

00:54:19.800 --> 00:54:25.130
Choose the adjustment that matters most
for access. It may be a small change in

00:54:25.131 --> 00:54:30.188
where information sits, when it is
offered, or how a student can respond. A

00:54:30.189 --> 00:54:35.724
tick on the page is a starting point. Use
and feedback will tell you more than the

00:54:35.725 --> 00:54:41.260
appearance of the resource. Keep that in
mind as we turn your brief into a plan to

00:54:41.261 --> 00:54:41.532
try.

00:54:43.041 --> 00:54:48.294
Page sixteen is My design. We're going to
pull the change together in five parts.

00:54:48.295 --> 00:54:53.351
First, state the barrier or unnecessary
demand. Second, describe the change to

00:54:53.352 --> 00:54:58.539
the environment, task or routine. Third,
explain how students' communication and

00:54:58.540 --> 00:55:03.531
experience will be included. Fourth,
decide what you will observe. Fifth, set

00:55:03.532 --> 00:55:08.128
a time to review. Make the measure about
access. Can a student find the

00:55:08.129 --> 00:55:13.054
information they need, communicate a
preference or participate in a way that

00:55:13.055 --> 00:55:17.913
works for them? Finishing faster or
appearing quieter does not, on its own,

00:55:17.914 --> 00:55:23.102
tell us the experience improved. Keep the
change small enough to try within your

00:55:23.103 --> 00:55:27.896
role. If you are watching with a
colleague, you can pause and explain your

00:55:27.897 --> 00:55:32.690
design in a couple of minutes, then invite
one question about the barrier,

00:55:32.691 --> 00:55:37.813
the change or the review. On your own,
read it back using those same questions.

00:55:37.814 --> 00:55:43.133
The original activity allows around seven
minutes of design time and a pair check.

00:55:43.134 --> 00:55:47.730
Pause now for the amount of time you want,
then resume for the debrief.

00:55:55.761 --> 00:56:00.606
Look at the connection between your first
and second steps. Does the change

00:56:00.607 --> 00:56:05.713
actually address the barrier you named? If
not, revise either the understanding

00:56:05.714 --> 00:56:10.821
of the barrier or the proposed support.
Now check the review. Who will help you

00:56:10.822 --> 00:56:15.667
find out whether access has improved? What
will the student's communication

00:56:15.668 --> 00:56:20.512
contribute? An adult observation is one
part of the evidence, not the whole

00:56:20.513 --> 00:56:25.817
account. Keep a one-sentence description
of your change and the access question it

00:56:25.818 --> 00:56:30.662
addresses. The design remains open to
refinement; you are not committing to

00:56:30.663 --> 00:56:33.608
keep a support that turns out to be
unhelpful.

00:56:35.120 --> 00:56:39.968
Turn to My students check on page
seventeen. Hold the same design up

00:56:39.969 --> 00:56:45.757
against Student A, Student B and Student
C. For each, consider the columns Helps,

00:56:45.758 --> 00:56:51.474
No difference and Needs something more.
This is a private thinking check. If you

00:56:51.475 --> 00:56:57.336
haven't tried the design yet, your answer
is a working expectation to test, rather

00:56:57.337 --> 00:57:03.125
than a confirmed result. Add a question
wherever you are unsure. Does one student

00:57:03.126 --> 00:57:08.770
need information in another form? Might
another need a different way to respond

00:57:08.771 --> 00:57:14.270
or more individual support? Keep the focus
on what the design offers and what

00:57:14.271 --> 00:57:19.987
still needs to be understood. Pause to
make those notes. You don't need to share

00:57:19.988 --> 00:57:24.763
student details, or turn the check into a
ranking of your students.

00:57:32.801 --> 00:57:37.876
A different result for each student is
useful information. It reminds us why

00:57:37.877 --> 00:57:42.613
we've held several students in mind across
the series. Where the design

00:57:42.614 --> 00:57:47.757
helps, it may be doing valuable work.
Where it makes no difference or someone

00:57:47.758 --> 00:57:52.697
needs more, that is a reason to consider
targeted support or an individual

00:57:52.698 --> 00:57:57.366
adjustment. Universal design and
individual support can work together.

00:57:58.880 --> 00:58:03.808
The continuum of support helps us keep
that whole picture in view. An inclusive

00:58:03.809 --> 00:58:08.928
environment might have clear routines and
a classroom culture where different ways

00:58:08.929 --> 00:58:13.541
of participating are accepted and valued.
Universal supports could include

00:58:13.542 --> 00:58:18.281
instructions available in different forms,
flexible ways to show learning or

00:58:18.282 --> 00:58:22.958
movement breaks that any student can
access. Targeted support responds to a

00:58:22.959 --> 00:58:27.255
more particular demand. A small group
might benefit from pre-teaching

00:58:27.256 --> 00:58:31.805
vocabulary or additional support to
organise independent work. Individual

00:58:31.806 --> 00:58:36.608
adjustments respond to a particular
student's access and participation needs.

00:58:36.609 --> 00:58:41.348
The examples in the guide include a
quieter assessment workspace, additional

00:58:41.349 --> 00:58:46.341
processing time, a visual task breakdown
or another way to demonstrate learning.

00:58:46.342 --> 00:58:51.144
These layers are connected. We can ask
what is available to everyone, what is

00:58:51.145 --> 00:58:55.821
targeted, and what is individually
adjusted. None of that should be decided

00:58:55.822 --> 00:59:00.498
from diagnosis alone. Consultation, the
day map and evidence from using the

00:59:00.499 --> 00:59:04.858
support help us understand what is needed.
Review remains part of each

00:59:04.859 --> 00:59:05.427
response.

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:11.951
Reasonable adjustments are changes or
supports that help students access and

00:59:11.952 --> 00:59:17.009
participate in education. They should
respond to the student and the barriers

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:21.867
they experience, and be practical and
effective in that context. The slide

00:59:21.868 --> 00:59:27.125
places environmental design and Universal
Design for Learning alongside the NCCD

00:59:27.126 --> 00:59:32.383
levels of adjustment. Those levels are
quality differentiated teaching practice,

00:59:32.384 --> 00:59:37.441
supplementary adjustments, substantial
adjustments and extensive adjustments.

00:59:37.442 --> 00:59:42.765
Keep the distinction clear: adjustments
are the actions and supports. The NCCD is

00:59:42.766 --> 00:59:47.823
the process for collecting information
about adjustments and the evidence for

00:59:47.824 --> 00:59:53.014
them. It does not replace the work of
listening, trying a support and reviewing

00:59:53.015 --> 00:59:58.073
what happens. For decisions about an
individual student, follow the processes

00:59:58.074 --> 01:00:03.330
in your school or system and work with the
relevant team. Here, we're connecting

01:00:03.331 --> 01:00:08.322
the design work to those processes, rather
than making a determination about

01:00:08.323 --> 01:00:09.652
any student's level.

01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:16.520
The Nationally Consistent Collection of
Data on School Students with Disability,

01:00:16.520 --> 01:00:21.202
or NCCD, connects adjustments with
evidence. Use current official NCCD

01:00:21.202 --> 01:00:26.088
information and the consultation,
documentation and review processes used

01:00:26.088 --> 01:00:30.770
by your school or system. The line on this
slide states that under the

01:00:30.770 --> 01:00:36.130
Disability Standards for Education 2005,
schools must consult the student, or an

01:00:36.130 --> 01:00:41.083
associate such as a parent or carer, about
adjustments. Keep the student's

01:00:41.083 --> 01:00:46.444
communication and experience central to
that consultation. Make responsibilities

01:00:46.444 --> 01:00:51.601
clear. Who will ask, who will put the
adjustment in place, what evidence will

01:00:51.601 --> 01:00:56.147
be gathered, and when will the team review
it? Take local procedural

01:00:56.147 --> 01:01:01.372
questions to the appropriate school or
system contact. Page twenty-four of the

01:01:01.372 --> 01:01:06.528
Participant Guide points to official NCCD
information. In Session Four, we'll

01:01:06.528 --> 01:01:10.462
develop the partnership and consultation
part of this work.

01:01:12.001 --> 01:01:17.151
Before we finish, turn to Commit on page
seventeen. What will you try? What will

01:01:17.152 --> 01:01:22.303
you notice? Who needs to be involved? How
will students and families help refine

01:01:22.304 --> 01:01:27.324
it? Write one thing under each of the
three headings in the guide: what I will

01:01:27.325 --> 01:01:32.215
try, what I will notice and who I will
involve. Keep it realistic and within

01:01:32.216 --> 01:01:37.236
your role. If a trial is not appropriate
yet, a question for consultation is a

01:01:37.237 --> 01:01:42.583
useful next step. You can share a general
commitment with colleagues if you choose,

01:01:42.584 --> 01:01:46.690
but private planning is enough. Pause here
to finish your notes.

01:01:54.721 --> 01:01:59.583
Keep the design as something to try,
review and refine. The student's and

01:01:59.584 --> 01:02:04.783
family's knowledge may change what you
notice, what you offer or how you judge

01:02:04.784 --> 01:02:09.916
its usefulness. That's part of a good
process. Thank you for your work today.

01:02:09.917 --> 01:02:15.319
Bring your design and your questions to
our fourth and final session, where we'll

01:02:15.320 --> 01:02:19.842
look at partnership, belonging and one
purposeful next conversation.

01:02:27.881 --> 01:02:32.679
Welcome to our fourth and final Primary
Education Online session. Today we're

01:02:32.680 --> 01:02:37.477
bringing the work on understanding and
classroom design into partnership with

01:02:37.478 --> 01:02:42.213
students and families. There is time built
in for your own planning, so keep

01:02:42.214 --> 01:02:47.264
your Participant Guide and your Session
Three design beside you. If you've had an

01:02:47.265 --> 01:02:52.063
opportunity to try or notice something
since last time, hold that in mind. If

01:02:52.064 --> 01:02:56.925
you haven't, you can work with the change
you proposed. If you're joining this

01:02:56.926 --> 01:03:01.913
session on its own, choose one familiar
routine and one small access question to

01:03:01.914 --> 01:03:07.028
plan a conversation around. We may bring
both productive and difficult partnership

01:03:07.029 --> 01:03:11.637
experiences to this learning. Sharing
remains voluntary, and examples stay

01:03:11.638 --> 01:03:16.562
anonymous. Listening and private notes are
enough. If you're with a live group,

01:03:16.563 --> 01:03:21.171
cameras are optional, and you can step
away when you need to. Our starting

01:03:21.172 --> 01:03:26.349
question comes from the last session: who
needs to be involved? Partnership is part

01:03:26.350 --> 01:03:30.388
of understanding and deciding what to do,
throughout the process.

01:03:31.921 --> 01:03:37.063
The diagram brings together family, school
staff and paraprofessionals, with

01:03:37.064 --> 01:03:42.479
the student at the centre. Every partner
has different knowledge and a different

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:47.348
relationship with the student. What is the
purpose of those intersecting

01:03:47.349 --> 01:03:52.764
partnerships? Take a moment to consider
that. It might help to think about whose

01:03:52.765 --> 01:03:57.907
experience should improve through the
decisions made. No single person holds

01:03:57.908 --> 01:04:02.844
the complete picture. A student can tell
or show us something about their

01:04:02.845 --> 01:04:08.261
preferences and experience. Families may
see things that don't appear at school.

01:04:08.262 --> 01:04:13.403
School staff and other people involved may
notice different contexts or hold

01:04:13.404 --> 01:04:18.683
useful knowledge about support. We want
that knowledge to come together around

01:04:18.684 --> 01:04:23.963
the student's access, participation and
wellbeing. Ask who is missing from the

01:04:23.964 --> 01:04:28.831
conversation, and how the student's
communication is included. A meeting

01:04:28.832 --> 01:04:33.768
about a child is not automatically a
meeting that has heard them. The key

01:04:33.769 --> 01:04:37.539
ideas on page eighteen keep this shared
purpose in view.

01:04:39.041 --> 01:04:44.354
Power is present in meeting structures,
language and decision pathways. It can be

01:04:44.355 --> 01:04:49.469
shaped by socio-economic circumstances,
cultural and historical experiences of

01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:54.784
education, language, and familiarity with
school systems and curriculum. A school

01:04:54.785 --> 01:04:59.899
team may be very comfortable with its
acronyms, meeting format and procedures.

01:04:59.900 --> 01:05:04.549
A family may have useful knowledge but
little access to those routines.

01:05:04.550 --> 01:05:09.066
Intentions matter, but so does the
experience of the person trying to

01:05:09.067 --> 01:05:14.248
contribute. Ask quietly: who sets the
agenda? Whose knowledge is heard? Who can

01:05:14.249 --> 01:05:19.495
make decisions? Is the student included in
a way that works for them? What could

01:05:19.496 --> 01:05:24.278
the school change about the process?
Online and phone meetings have these

01:05:24.279 --> 01:05:29.393
questions too. Who can join easily? Is a
camera expected? Who does most of the

01:05:29.394 --> 01:05:33.778
talking? Do people have information
beforehand and a way to respond

01:05:33.779 --> 01:05:38.561
afterwards? We can examine those
conditions without labelling a family as

01:05:38.562 --> 01:05:40.153
difficult or disengaged.

01:05:41.681 --> 01:05:46.597
Let's use the Power dynamics activity on
page nineteen. There are three columns:

01:05:46.598 --> 01:05:51.577
an imbalance from a home perspective, an
imbalance from a school perspective, and

01:05:51.578 --> 01:05:56.246
how you could address these imbalances.
Think about a process rather than an

01:05:56.247 --> 01:06:00.665
identifying incident. From home, there
might be uncertainty about school

01:06:00.666 --> 01:06:05.769
language or who can make a decision. From
school, there may be information the team

01:06:05.770 --> 01:06:10.127
has not understood or a process that
limits the conversation. Those are

01:06:10.128 --> 01:06:14.484
examples to help you begin; use a
situation you know without naming the

01:06:14.485 --> 01:06:18.717
people involved. Then choose a process
change. Could questions go out

01:06:18.718 --> 01:06:23.261
beforehand? Could a meeting time or
communication method change? Could the

01:06:23.262 --> 01:06:28.178
agenda include something the family or
student wants to understand? Your private

01:06:28.179 --> 01:06:33.158
notes are enough. If you are watching in a
live group with a shared whiteboard or

01:06:33.159 --> 01:06:37.951
chat, you can use the headings Home,
School and Solutions. There is no need to

01:06:37.952 --> 01:06:42.806
share a sensitive story. Pause for a few
minutes, and bring back one change you

01:06:42.807 --> 01:06:43.490
could make.

01:06:51.521 --> 01:06:56.402
Look at your Solutions column. Does the
change give someone a clearer or more

01:06:56.403 --> 01:07:00.963
accessible way to contribute? Does it
leave room for knowledge you don't

01:07:00.964 --> 01:07:05.652
already have? A useful change can be quite
small. Explaining an acronym or

01:07:05.653 --> 01:07:10.405
sending a clear purpose in advance can
change how a conversation begins. It

01:07:10.406 --> 01:07:15.287
won't remove every imbalance, but it is
something we can examine and improve.

01:07:15.288 --> 01:07:19.848
Keep one process change for the
conversation plan later in this session.

01:07:21.361 --> 01:07:25.680
We're going to hear from Adon in the
supplied student voice film. As you

01:07:25.681 --> 01:07:30.425
watch, pay attention to what decisions
were made and who made them. What impact

01:07:30.426 --> 01:07:34.501
did those decisions have on him? Let's
listen to his account without

01:07:34.502 --> 01:07:35.290
interrupting.

01:09:27.721 --> 01:09:33.428
Take a moment with the questions on page
nineteen. What decisions were made, and

01:09:33.429 --> 01:09:38.775
who made them? What impact did those
decisions have on Adon? Then bring the

01:09:38.776 --> 01:09:44.338
question back to your setting. How are the
students you have in mind asked and

01:09:44.339 --> 01:09:50.119
heard? Write a brief reflection, or share
an observation from the film if you are

01:09:50.120 --> 01:09:54.237
watching with colleagues. Pause here if
you'd like longer.

01:10:02.241 --> 01:10:06.701
The point to carry forward is the
importance of asking students in a way

01:10:06.702 --> 01:10:11.727
that works for them, and acting on what
they communicate. Even young children can

01:10:11.728 --> 01:10:16.187
express preferences when choices and
support are accessible. What helps?

01:10:16.188 --> 01:10:21.151
What's hard? What would you like teachers
to know? The question, the setting and

01:10:21.152 --> 01:10:25.863
the way to respond all matter. Where
possible, record the student's words or

01:10:25.864 --> 01:10:30.700
communication accurately, so their view
remains part of the record rather than

01:10:30.701 --> 01:10:35.098
being replaced by an adult summary. That
can support the development of

01:10:35.099 --> 01:10:36.605
self-advocacy over time.

01:10:38.120 --> 01:10:43.140
Consultation literacy means being able to
ask, listen and follow up in a useful

01:10:43.141 --> 01:10:48.032
way. The checklist on page twenty begins
with a clear shared purpose and open

01:10:48.033 --> 01:10:53.053
questions. It includes more than one way
to contribute, respect for student and

01:10:53.054 --> 01:10:58.267
family knowledge, and an accurate record
of what was heard. It ends with an agreed

01:10:58.268 --> 01:11:02.902
action, responsibility and follow-up. That
final part helps distinguish a

01:11:02.903 --> 01:11:08.116
conversation that felt positive from one
that leads to something people understand

01:11:08.117 --> 01:11:12.944
and can review. Use the checklist before a
meeting and again afterwards. Did

01:11:12.945 --> 01:11:17.707
people have a real opportunity to
influence the discussion? Does the record

01:11:17.708 --> 01:11:22.792
reflect their contribution? Is it clear
what happens next? A tool should support

01:11:22.793 --> 01:11:24.466
listening, not replace it.

01:11:26.000 --> 01:11:31.043
Let's look at students and families in
turn. With students, offer communication

01:11:31.044 --> 01:11:36.282
choices. That might involve AAC, drawing,
writing, symbols, a video or a supported

01:11:36.283 --> 01:11:40.938
conversation. Prepare them with
information about what will be discussed.

01:11:40.939 --> 01:11:45.788
Offer manageable choices and enough
processing time. Think about the sensory

01:11:45.789 --> 01:11:50.574
conditions of the space. A trusted
supporter can assist communication while

01:11:50.575 --> 01:11:55.230
keeping the student's voice central.
Notice preferences expressed through

01:11:55.231 --> 01:12:00.275
actions as well as words, and check your
interpretation. Build questions around

01:12:00.276 --> 01:12:05.319
strengths, interests and what matters to
the student. With families, begin with

01:12:05.320 --> 01:12:10.234
the student's strengths and what works
well. Listen to the family's knowledge

01:12:10.235 --> 01:12:15.343
without assuming that their experience
matches the school's. Explain educational

01:12:15.344 --> 01:12:19.611
language and use real, respectful
examples. Families have different

01:12:19.612 --> 01:12:24.656
cultural experiences, and some parents are
neurodivergent themselves. Check for

01:12:24.657 --> 01:12:29.829
shared understanding. Agree on next steps
and keep communication open. Choose one

01:12:29.830 --> 01:12:34.679
practice from each column that you would
like to strengthen. You can note it

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:37.330
privately and carry it into your planning.

01:12:38.840 --> 01:12:44.133
This slide touches on distress and times
when capacity runs out. Please look after

01:12:44.134 --> 01:12:49.426
yourself as you listen, and step away if
you need to. The terms here are meltdown,

01:12:49.427 --> 01:12:54.132
shutdown and School Can't. We're
discussing them without treating them as

01:12:54.133 --> 01:12:58.903
wilful misbehaviour or a failure of
parenting. The experiences and demands

01:12:58.904 --> 01:13:04.131
leading up to a difficult point may not
all be visible at school. Home and school

01:13:04.132 --> 01:13:09.294
can observe different parts of the same
student's day. Our focus is safety, what

01:13:09.295 --> 01:13:14.195
preceded the situation, what supports
recovery, and what could change in the

01:13:14.196 --> 01:13:18.901
environment, task or response. What
information is missing? What does the

01:13:18.902 --> 01:13:23.868
student communicate? What can home and
school learn from each other? Keep the

01:13:23.869 --> 01:13:28.769
discussion within respectful educational
practice. There is no need to put a

01:13:28.770 --> 01:13:33.801
personal crisis story into a group chat. A
concern about an individual student

01:13:33.802 --> 01:13:38.834
belongs with the appropriate support
people and school processes. Today we are

01:13:38.835 --> 01:13:43.343
considering how partnership helps us
listen and respond without blame.

01:13:44.880 --> 01:13:48.938
Now open Bridge to the next family
conversation on page twenty. Bring back

01:13:48.939 --> 01:13:52.997
your Session Three design and one question
that still needs other people's

01:13:52.998 --> 01:13:56.722
knowledge. What needs to be understood?
What question could open the

01:13:56.723 --> 01:14:00.948
conversation? How will the student's voice
be included? What could be decided

01:14:00.949 --> 01:14:05.285
together? How and when will the school
follow up? Keep your process change from

01:14:05.286 --> 01:14:09.232
the power activity beside you. If you
wanted information to be available

01:14:09.233 --> 01:14:13.291
earlier, or a different way for someone to
contribute, include that in the

01:14:13.292 --> 01:14:17.795
preparation. You are planning to listen,
not scripting what the family should say.

01:14:17.796 --> 01:14:22.021
A few notes are enough here; we'll use
them in the more detailed conversation

01:14:22.022 --> 01:14:25.746
plan shortly. Pause the video for a few
minutes to think it through.

01:14:33.760 --> 01:14:37.898
Look at the purpose you've written. Does
it identify something you need to

01:14:37.899 --> 01:14:42.036
understand? Does the opening question
leave room for an answer you haven't

01:14:42.037 --> 01:14:46.061
predicted? Keep these notes open. They
will help you make the later plan

01:14:46.062 --> 01:14:50.143
specific while leaving the actual
conversation open to student and family

01:14:50.144 --> 01:14:50.710
knowledge.

01:14:52.240 --> 01:14:57.327
Belonging, capacity and joy help us judge
the direction of our work. Belonging

01:14:57.328 --> 01:15:02.349
means being recognised, included and able
to participate as oneself. Capacity

01:15:02.350 --> 01:15:07.504
brings us back to what the day costs, what
protects resources and what restores

01:15:07.505 --> 01:15:12.592
them. Joy includes interests, connection
and authentic expression. These ideas

01:15:12.593 --> 01:15:17.747
connect the lens from Session One, the
school-day map and the classroom design.

01:15:17.748 --> 01:15:22.769
They also give partnership a purpose
beyond agreeing on a procedure. Consider

01:15:22.770 --> 01:15:27.857
your proposed change. Does it reduce a
barrier while also protecting something

01:15:27.858 --> 01:15:33.011
the student values? Is there room for
their interests or their preferred way to

01:15:33.012 --> 01:15:38.100
connect? Quiet compliance or academic
performance alone cannot tell us whether

01:15:38.101 --> 01:15:42.395
a student is flourishing. We need their
experience in the picture.

01:15:43.920 --> 01:15:49.484
Turn to Start, Continue, Reconsider on
page twenty-one. Choose what matters most

01:15:49.485 --> 01:15:54.909
now. You don't need to summarise every
topic we've covered. Under Start, write

01:15:54.910 --> 01:16:00.403
one small strategy you could begin next
week. Your Session Three commitment can

01:16:00.404 --> 01:16:06.039
be that starting point. Under Continue,
name a practice the series has helped you

01:16:06.040 --> 01:16:11.182
recognise as useful. Under Reconsider,
choose an approach you would now do

01:16:11.183 --> 01:16:16.817
differently. For each, ask how you will
know whether it helps. What evidence will

01:16:16.818 --> 01:16:21.396
show a difference in access, participation
or experience? For your

01:16:21.397 --> 01:16:26.961
Start item, it may help to finish these
sentences: I will. The barrier or demand

01:16:26.962 --> 01:16:32.597
I am addressing is. I will notice. I need
to consult. I will review this on. Keep

01:16:32.598 --> 01:16:38.021
the change within your influence and make
sure it doesn't require a student to

01:16:38.022 --> 01:16:43.446
appear less Autistic. This plan belongs to
you. Pause for around five to seven

01:16:43.447 --> 01:16:46.334
minutes if you'd like to complete it now.

01:16:54.360 --> 01:16:59.341
Read your Start back and check that it is
small, testable and open to revision. If

01:16:59.342 --> 01:17:04.077
your measure is only that a student will
sit quietly or finish quickly, add an

01:17:04.078 --> 01:17:08.998
access question. Can they understand the
task? Can they communicate a preference?

01:17:08.999 --> 01:17:13.734
What do they tell or show us about the
support? Continue matters too. We don't

01:17:13.735 --> 01:17:18.286
need to discard a routine that offers
safety, connection or joy. Reconsider

01:17:18.287 --> 01:17:22.222
gives us permission to change our practice
as we understand more.

01:17:23.760 --> 01:17:28.825
Let's prepare the next conversation on
page twenty-two. Use the bridge notes you

01:17:28.826 --> 01:17:34.019
made earlier. Choose a real conversation
you could have within the next two weeks,

01:17:34.020 --> 01:17:38.829
with a student's family or with the
student themselves. Write the purpose in

01:17:38.830 --> 01:17:43.254
one sentence. What do you need to
understand? Then fill in what you'll

01:17:43.255 --> 01:17:48.191
share, beginning with a strength; what
you'll ask, as specifically as you can;

01:17:48.192 --> 01:17:53.129
and what you'll offer. Include how the
student's communication and preferences

01:17:53.130 --> 01:17:58.067
will be heard. Now write your opening
sentence, word for word. It should sound

01:17:58.068 --> 01:18:02.748
like an invitation to understand together.
Leave room for the other person

01:18:02.749 --> 01:18:07.943
to change the direction of the plan. Add
one possible shared next step and how you

01:18:07.944 --> 01:18:13.137
would follow up. Note when, where and how
the conversation could happen. If you're

01:18:13.138 --> 01:18:17.947
working with a colleague, you can pause
and read the opening sentence aloud,

01:18:17.948 --> 01:18:22.884
asking whether it sounds open. You can
also read it quietly to yourself. Pause

01:18:22.885 --> 01:18:26.731
for the time you need to plan, then return
for a final check.

01:18:34.760 --> 01:18:38.887
Check your opening sentence. Does it
communicate a clear purpose without

01:18:38.888 --> 01:18:42.841
asking the other person to endorse a
finished decision? Is your offer

01:18:42.842 --> 01:18:47.027
something you can realistically do? Have
you included a way to check that

01:18:47.028 --> 01:18:51.736
everyone's understanding is shared? Look
back at the process change from the power

01:18:51.737 --> 01:18:55.922
reflection. Make sure it appears somewhere
in the plan. The plan prepares

01:18:55.923 --> 01:19:00.166
you for a conversation; it doesn't control
what the student or family will

01:19:00.167 --> 01:19:04.527
say. Listening may change the next step,
and your follow-up should make that

01:19:04.528 --> 01:19:04.991
visible.

01:19:06.520 --> 01:19:11.395
For our final reflection, turn to page
twenty-three. Then look back at Think

01:19:11.396 --> 01:19:16.337
about your students on page four. Read
your own words about what you hoped to

01:19:16.338 --> 01:19:20.953
understand at the beginning. What's
different now? Where were you at the

01:19:20.954 --> 01:19:26.154
start of Session One, and where are you
now? If you don't have those first notes,

01:19:26.155 --> 01:19:31.290
think back to the question you brought
with you. Then answer the final prompt in

01:19:31.291 --> 01:19:36.362
one sentence, for yourself: what will you
do differently in your teaching? This

01:19:36.363 --> 01:19:41.302
reflection can stay entirely private.
Pause here for a quiet moment to write.

01:19:49.320 --> 01:19:53.372
You may have a clearer next step, a
different question, or a better sense of

01:19:53.373 --> 01:19:57.372
what you still need to understand. The
reflection doesn't need to show that

01:19:57.373 --> 01:20:01.695
every question has been answered. Keep it
with your action and conversation plans

01:20:01.696 --> 01:20:04.830
so you can return to it after trying
something in practice.

01:20:06.360 --> 01:20:10.787
The series is one point in continuing
professional learning. What are you

01:20:10.788 --> 01:20:15.707
curious to explore next? Look back at the
questions you began with and choose the

01:20:15.708 --> 01:20:20.565
one that would be most useful to follow.
Page twenty-four points to the Positive

01:20:20.566 --> 01:20:25.362
Partnerships website and further learning
pathways. The supplied materials name

01:20:25.363 --> 01:20:30.344
webinars and deep dives on topics such as
Universal Design for Learning, executive

01:20:30.345 --> 01:20:34.894
functioning and sensory design, as well as
an Online Community of Practice.

01:20:34.895 --> 01:20:39.814
Specific titles, dates and the community
access link are still to be confirmed in

01:20:39.815 --> 01:20:44.488
this package, so check with the delivery
team for those details. The Positive

01:20:44.489 --> 01:20:48.978
Partnerships website is a place to explore
available resources. Choose the

01:20:48.979 --> 01:20:53.898
next resource because it helps answer a
real question in your practice. You don't

01:20:53.899 --> 01:20:56.050
need to take on everything at once.

01:20:57.560 --> 01:21:02.194
Before we close, make a little space for
feedback. It helps the team understand

01:21:02.195 --> 01:21:06.948
what supported learning and what could be
clearer or more useful. The link and QR

01:21:06.949 --> 01:21:10.811
code on the slide lead to the Professional
Learning feedback form,

01:21:10.812 --> 01:21:15.149
which includes an online delivery option.
If you're completing it for this

01:21:15.150 --> 01:21:19.428
workshop, check with your delivery team
that you are choosing the correct

01:21:19.429 --> 01:21:24.063
workshop or event. If the options don't
match, ask the team for the appropriate

01:21:24.064 --> 01:21:28.877
way to provide feedback. If you're using
this simulation simply to become familiar

01:21:28.878 --> 01:21:33.572
with the content, you can instead note one
thing that helped and one thing you'd

01:21:33.573 --> 01:21:37.969
like clarified, ready to share through
your team's usual process. Pause the

01:21:37.970 --> 01:21:41.119
video while you complete the form or make
those notes.

01:21:49.120 --> 01:21:53.268
Thank you for taking a moment to consider
the learning experience. Keep any

01:21:53.268 --> 01:21:57.752
questions that still need a response with
your notes, so they can be taken to the

01:21:57.752 --> 01:22:00.497
appropriate member of the delivery or
school team.

01:22:02.000 --> 01:22:05.994
Thank you for your attention and
reflection across these four online

01:22:05.995 --> 01:22:10.346
sessions. Keep your Start, Continue,
Reconsider plan and your conversation

01:22:10.347 --> 01:22:14.938
plan somewhere you can return to them. The
students you brought to mind at the

01:22:14.939 --> 01:22:19.409
beginning remain at the centre. Keep
noticing their strengths and what gives

01:22:19.410 --> 01:22:23.702
them joy. Keep asking how they experience
the school day and how they can

01:22:23.703 --> 01:22:28.174
communicate what matters to them. Take one
thoughtful next step, include the

01:22:28.175 --> 01:22:32.646
people who hold relevant knowledge, and
review what happens. Small, informed

01:22:32.647 --> 01:22:37.297
changes can be refined through student
voice, observation and partnership. Stay

01:22:37.298 --> 01:22:39.801
curious, and good luck with the work
ahead.

